      "text": "We are given that the set of three numbers has both a mean and median equal to 4, and the smallest number in the set is 1.\n\nLet's denote the numbers in the set as A, B, and C where A ≤ B ≤ C.\n\nSince the mean of the set is 4, the sum of the numbers can be calculated as follows:\nMean = (Sum of numbers) / (Number of numbers)\n4 = (A + B + C) / 3\nMultiplying both sides by 3 to eliminate the denominator, we get:\nA + B + C = 12\n\nWe know that A is 1, so we can substitute it into the equation to find the sum of B and C:\n1 + B + C = 12\nB + C = 11\n\nSince the mean is 4 and the median is also 4, B and C must be equal to each other. This is because, in an ordered list with an odd number of elements (in this case, 3), the median is the middle number. If B and C are not equal, then the median would be either B or C, depending on their values. However, we are given that the median is exactly 4, which implies B = C.\n\nNow, we can calculate the range of the set:\nRange = Largest number - Smallest number\nRange = C - A\nSince B = C, we can substitute C for B, so the range becomes:\nRange = C - 1\n\nTo find the value of C (and thus the range), we can use the equation B + C = 11 and the fact that B = C:\nC + C = 11\n2C = 11\nC = 11 / 2\n\nSo, the largest number in the set is 11 / 2, and the range is:\nRange = C - A\nRange = (11 / 2) - 1\nRange = 11 / 2 - 2/2\nRange = (11 - 2) / 2\nRange = 9 / 2\n\nTherefore, the range of the set of numbers is \\boxed{9}.\n\nNow, let's eliminate the wrong answers.\n\nA. 5: This is not the correct range because 5 is less than the smallest number (1).\n\nB. 6: This is not the correct range because 6 is greater than the smallest number (1).\n\nC. 9: This is the correct range based on our calculations.\n\nD. 12: This cannot be the correct range because the sum of the numbers must be at least 12 (as calculated earlier).\n\nE. 15: This cannot be the correct range because the largest number cannot exceed the sum of the other two numbers plus the smallest number.\n\nSo, the correct answer is C: 9.\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ if $|3x - 5| = |x + 3| + 2$?\n\nTo solve the equation \\( |3x - 5| = |x + 3| + 2 \\), we need to consider the different cases based on the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign. These critical points are \\( x = \\frac{5}{3} \\) and \\( x = -3 \\). We will consider three intervals: \\( x < -3 \\), \\( -3 \\leq x < \\frac{5}{3} \\), and \\( x \\geq \\frac{5}{3} \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( x < -3 \\)**\n\nIn this interval, \\( 3x - 5 < 0 \\) and \\( x + 3 < 0 \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |3x - 5| = -(3x - 5) = -3x + 5 \\]\n\\[ |x + 3| = -(x + 3) = -x - 3 \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ -3x + 5 = -x - 3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ -3x + 5 = -x - 1 \\]\n\\[ -3x + x = -1 - 5 \\]\n\\[ -2x = -6 \\]\n\\[ x = 3 \\]\nHowever, \\( x = 3 \\) does not lie in the interval \\( x < -3 \\), so there is no solution in this interval.\n\n**Case 2: \\( -3 \\leq x < \\frac{5}{3} \\)**\n\nIn this interval, \\( 3x - 5 < 0 \\) and \\( x + 3 \\geq 0 \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |3x - 5| = -(3x - 5) = -3x + 5 \\]\n\\[ |x + 3| = x + 3 \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ -3x + 5 = x + 3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ -3x + 5 = x + 5 \\]\n\\[ -3x - x = 5 - 5 \\]\n\\[ -4x = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = 0 \\]\nWe need to verify if \\( x = 0 \\) satisfies the original equation within the interval \\( -3 \\leq x < \\frac{5}{3} \\). Substituting \\( x = 0 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ |3(0) - 5| = |0 + 3| + 2 \\]\n\\[ |-5| = |3| + 2 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 5 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 7 \\]\nSince the left side does not equal the right side, there is no solution in this interval.\n\n**Case 3: \\( x \\geq \\frac{5}{3} \\)**\n\nIn this interval, \\( 3x - 5 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( x + 3 \\geq 0 \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |3x - 5| = 3x - 5 \\]\n\\[ |x + 3| = x + 3 \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ 3x - 5 = x + 3 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 3x - 5 = x + 5 \\]\n\\[ 3x - x = 5 + 5 \\]\n\\[ 2x = 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 5 \\]\nWe need to verify if \\( x = 5 \\) satisfies the original equation within the interval \\( x \\geq \\frac{5}{3} \\). Substituting \\( x = 5 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ |3(5) - 5| = |5 + 3| + 2 \\]\n\\[ |15 - 5| = |8| + 2 \\]\n\\[ |10| = 8 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 10 = 10 \\]\nSince both sides are equal, \\( x = 5 \\) is a solution.\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).\n\nGiven that the terminal side of angle \\\\(\\alpha\\\\) intersects the unit circle at point \\\\(P\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},{-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\right)\\\\).  \n\\\\((1)\\\\) Find the value of \\\\(\\tan \\alpha\\\\).  \n\\\\((2)\\\\) Find the value of \\\\(\\dfrac{\\cos (\\dfrac{\\pi }{2}+\\alpha )\\sin (\\pi +\\alpha)}{\\cos (\\dfrac{11\\pi }{2}-\\alpha )\\sin (\\dfrac{9\\pi }{2}+\\alpha )}\\\\).\n\nGiven that the terminal side of angle \\(\\alpha\\) intersects the unit circle at the point \\(P\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\), we can identify the coordinates of the point as \\((x, y) = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\).\n\n### Part (1): Finding \\(\\tan \\alpha\\)\n\nThe tangent of an angle in the unit circle is given by the ratio of the y-coordinate to the x-coordinate of the point where the terminal side intersects the unit circle. Therefore,\n\\[\n\\tan \\alpha = \\frac{y}{x} = \\frac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1.\n\\]\n\n### Part (2): Evaluating the Expression\n\nWe need to find the value of the expression \\(\\frac{\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right) \\sin(\\pi + \\alpha)}{\\cos \\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{2} - \\alpha\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right)}\\).\n\nFirst, we use trigonometric identities to simplify the expression:\n\\[\n\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\alpha\\right) = -\\sin \\alpha,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\left(\\pi + \\alpha\\right) = -\\sin \\alpha,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos \\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{2} - \\alpha\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\alpha\\right) = \\sin \\alpha,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin